  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 AM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nUpper trough seen on water vapor imagery early this morning \nover South Dakota will move east to the northern IL/southern WI \nby later this afternoon. Yesterday's cold front has pushed the \nmoist and unstable air mass south into Missouri and downstate \nIllinois. As large scale ascent with this trough and the right \nentrance region of a 100+ kt upper level jet overspread the \nmoist/unstable air mass over Missouri, an uptick in the coverage\nand intensity of showers and thunderstorms is expected this \nmorning. The majority of this rain should remain south of our \nCWA, though it still looks like some showers could affect areas \nsouth of Hwy 24 today. \n\nConsiderable cloudiness is expected much of the day, primarily \nmid-high level cloudiness across much of the CWA. This should \nkeep temps in the mid 70s to near 80 degrees for highs today. \nCoolest reading should be south of the Kankakee and Illinois \nRiver Valleys where thicker cloud cover will be more effective \nat limiting insolation. \n\nSubsidence in the wake of this trough should result in skies \nclearing out from NW to SE starting late this afternoon with \nmost of the area likely to be mostly clear by mid evening. The \nclearing skies, light winds, and dry air mass should allow lows \nto dip into the upper 40s tonight in the typically colder low \nlying areas. Urban heat island should keep Chicago in the lower \nto middle 60s for lows tonight. \n\nSunny skies, light winds, comfortable humidity, and seasonable \nmild/warm temperatures will make for a gorgeous early autumn day\non Friday. The upcoming weekend continues to look by large dry \nwith guidance trending even less with rain chances over the past\n12-24 hours. Warm air advection should result in highs warming \na bit Saturday into the low to mid 80s. Weakening cold front \nwill move across the area Saturday night, but given the \nantecedent dry air mass and stronger forcing remaining well \nnorth of the area, the local impact should be some slightly \ncooler temps for Sunday and a wind shift to northerly. \n\nThe first half of next week continues to look like it will turn\nmore active as we get into more of a southwesterly flow aloft. \nThere will be multiple opportunities for showers and \nthunderstorms Monday-Wednesday as several shortwave troughs \nripple through the southwesterly flow. Too early to pinpoint \ntiming or rainfall amounts, but there is fairly high confidence \nin a transition to a generally wetter, more unsettled pattern \nnext week.\n\n- Izzi",
